 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 66,
   "y": 36,
   "plaque": "galway_ballinasloe",
   "title": "Ballinasloe Fair",
   "text": "Every October Ballinasloe, east of here in County Galway, holds one of the oldest horse fairs in Europe, and Irish Travellers have long been at its heart. Travellers, who call themselves Mincéirí and have their own language, Cant, were recognised by the Irish State as an ethnic minority in 2017.",
   "people": "travellers"
  },
  {
   "text": "BROWNE DOORWAY. A carved stone doorway of 1627 from the house of the Brownes, one of the Fourteen Tribes of Galway. It's been rehomed in a park, which is a very Galway thing to do.",
   "x": 29.0,
   "y": 10.0
  },
  {
   "text": "GALWAY CATHEDRAL. Finished in 1965 on the site of the old city jail. A big stone dome, a lot of Connemara marble and a rose window you could lose a day in.",
   "x": 44.0,
   "y": 15.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE CLADDAGH. An old fishing village outside the walls, with its own king. It gave the world the Claddagh ring: two hands holding a heart under a crown, for love, loyalty and friendship.",
   "x": 36.0,
   "y": 33.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE SPANISH ARCH. Built in 1584 as part of the city walls to guard the quay where Spanish merchants unloaded wine. Galway's been trading with Spain for ages. The arch now guards a lot of ice-cream.",
   "x": 46.0,
   "y": 39.0
  },
  {
   "text": "GALWAY DOCKS. A working harbour since the Middle Ages. The Galway hooker, a sturdy black-hulled sailing boat with red-brown sails, still races in Cruinniú na mBád in summer. They're slower than the gossip.",
   "x": 61.0,
   "y": 35.0
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 9,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: Nora Barnacle, the wife of James Joyce, was born in Galway in 1884.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "galway_nora_barnacle",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Nora Barnacle"
  },
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 14,
   "plaque": "galway_salmon_weir",
   "title": "Salmon Weir Bridge",
   "text": "Beside the cathedral, the Salmon Weir Bridge crosses the Corrib just below the weir. In spring and early summer you can look down and see wild salmon lined up in the clear water, waiting to swim up to Lough Corrib. They queue better than most people."
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 23,
   "plaque": "galway_st_nicholas",
   "title": "St Nicholas' Church",
   "text": "Founded in 1320, St Nicholas' Collegiate Church is the largest medieval parish church in Ireland still in use. Nicholas is the patron saint of sailors, which in a port town is just good sense. Cromwell's soldiers stabled horses inside."
  },
  {
   "x": 65,
   "y": 34,
   "plaque": "galway_dun_aonghasa",
   "title": "Dún Aonghasa",
   "text": "Out in Galway Bay lie the Aran Islands: Inis Mór, Inis Meáin and Inis Oírr. On Inis Mór, Dún Aonghasa is a prehistoric stone fort on the very edge of a cliff about 100 metres above the Atlantic. There's no wall on the sea side. The sea is the wall."
  },
  {
   "x": 13,
   "y": 33,
   "plaque": "galway_races",
   "title": "The Galway Races",
   "text": "Racing has been held at Ballybrit, on the edge of town, since 1869. Race week, at the end of July, is a seven-day festival of horses, hats and optimism. Some people come for the horses. Most come for the hats."
  },
  {
   "text": "SOUTH TO CLARE, THE SHANNON AND LIMERICK. The old coast road. In real life it goes the long way round the bay by Oranmore and Ennis; the Wildlore causeway is a shortcut the Atlantic puts up with, mostly.",
   "x": 33,
   "y": 40
  },
  {
   "text": "EYRE SQUARE. A few acres of green in the middle of Galway, given to the city in 1710 and now officially the John F. Kennedy Memorial Park. Kennedy visited in 1963 and the town's never stopped telling people.",
   "x": 24.0,
   "y": 10.0
  }
 ],
